#   (Q1 -- 0011 withdrawn 22.09.: drivers/misc/sunxi-nsi.c is not an NSI driver.
#    It calls itself an ARM-MIPS interface "based on factory 0x3061000 base" and
#    invents a register map (CTRL 0x00, IRQ_EN 0x08, MEM_BASE 0x10, MEM_SIZE 0x14)
#    for nsi-controller@2020000, whose real layout is base + 0x200*port, +0x10 mode,
#    +0x14 priority, +0x18 io, port 0 = the CPU master (vendor nsi_port_setmode
#    @0xC05404C0 / nsi_port_setpri @0xC0540520, AP3s section 11). Its probe would
#    write a DMA address into the CPU master's MBUS mode register and a buffer
#    length into its priority register. Nothing calls into it - its three exports
#    have no user, the nsi@2020000 node carries another compatible, and the ARM-MIPS
#    IPC it claims to be is 0014/0092. The file stays in patches/kernel/ as history.
#    A real NSI driver, if one is ever wanted, starts from the vendor layout, not
#    from this file.)
